<title>UK: Industrial countries dominate insurance - Swiss Re.</title>
<headline>Industrial countries dominate insurance - Swiss Re.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-06-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Highly industrialised countries with increasingly significant service sectors continue to account for some 90 percent of global insurance premiums, according to reinsurers Swiss Re.</p>
<p>Out of total world insurance premiums of $2,143.4 billion in 1995, 30.8 per cent came from North America, 29.2 per cent from western Europe, and 29.7 per cent from Japan.</p>
<p>&quot;They thus represent an overproportionate share in relation to their population (15 per cent of the world total) as well as in terms of their share of gross domestic product (75 percent),&quot; Swiss Re said in its latest Sigma research report.</p>
<p>The table below shows the relative significance of the various economic regions. The picture is clearly dominated by the industrialised nations. The emerging regions such as South East Asia, Latin America, and Eastern Europe still account for only a small share.</p>
<p>1995 TOTAL INSURANCE BUSINESS ACCORDING TO REGIONS.</p>
<p>    REGION		    $  BILLION	    PER CENT</p>
<p>    North America		661.1		   30.8</p>
<p>    Latin America		 31.0		    1.4</p>
<p>    Europe			 638.4		   29.8</p>
<p>	 Western Europe	  626.4		   29.2</p>
<p>	 Eastern Europe	   12.0		    0.6</p>
<p>    Asia			   756.2		   35.3</p>
<p>	 Japan		     637.3		   29.7</p>
<p>	 South and East Asia   108.7		    5.1</p>
<p>	 Asia minor		 10.3		    0.5</p>
<p>    Africa			  25.1		    1.2</p>
<p>    Oceania			 31.6		    1.5</p>
<p>--- Gordon Mackenzie  Reuters Insurance News +44 171 542 2770</p>
</text>
